window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-26T05:45:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-24","event":"Printed: Ifo 90.6 (90.5 expected, October revised to 90.1; current 89.4, expectations 91.9); US Conference Board confidence 103.3 (92.2 expected); STOXX 600 −0.66% to 632.74","why":"The Ifo confirms German momentum but manufacturing slipped; the STOXX 600 is back under the 635 trigger."},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Printed: ECB non-policy meeting postponed the minimum reserve ratio decision (Handelsblatt: after December, possibly spring 2027); US core PCE 3.3% y/y (3.2% expected), Q3 GDP revised to 4.3%; German Q3 GDP revised to +0.1% q/q; INSEE confidence 82; USTR 15% Section 301 proposal on EU goods; STOXX 600 −1.06% to 626.03","why":"The liquidity step is deferred, the Fed leg is firm at 97.8% for 9 Dec, German hard data cooled and the trade risk is formal."},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Italian Treasury auctions: BTP Short Term and BTP€i on 25 Nov (results not confirmed on a page read), BOTs on 26 Nov, medium and long-term BTPs on 27 Nov; the BTP-Bund spread closed 111.9 bp","why":"First Italian supply after the spread widened to 107 bp; demand confirms or questions the calm periphery"},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Trump met NATO’s Rutte in Palm Beach late on 25 Nov on Ukraine security guarantees and the European role; no outcome was published on a page read in this run","why":"Any statement on Article 5-style guarantees or a US-led format without Europe moves defence names and the OAT"},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"European Council: Costa begins consultations on the ECB presidency (de Cos, Knot) and board seats (Brunnermeier; Portugal weighing a candidate) with Parliament and the Governing Council","why":"Sets the ECB’s 2027 leadership before Lagarde leaves on 7 Feb"},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"ECB account of the 29 Oct meeting (13:30 CET); German GfK consumer climate, December (08:00 CET); Italian BOT auction; constitutional deadline for the French social security budget; US markets closed for Thanksgiving","why":"The account shows how far the Council is from a 2027 pause; a holiday-thinned session tests the 625.65 low with no US lead"},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"US half session (stocks close 13:00 ET); Italian medium and long-term BTP auction; EC economic sentiment and final consumer confidence, November (ESI prior 96.9, flash confidence −16.9)","why":"The ESI against 97 is a regime input; the BTP auction tests demand with the spread at 111.9 bp"},{"date":"2026-11-28","event":"French Senate public vote on the first (revenue) part of the 2027 finance bill (may run into 29 Nov)","why":"Decides whether the Senate text reaches the CMP with a revenue side the Assemblée rejected on 4 Nov"},{"date":"2026-11-29","event":"Spanish general election, called by Sánchez on 5 Oct after parliament rejected his housing measures; polls point to a PP-led coalition with Vox","why":"Spain is the bloc’s growth engine; a PP-Vox government would be the first with the far right since 1975 and changes the fiscal and energy stance"},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"German CPI flash, November; German retail sales, October (prior −2.0% m/m); first session after the Spanish election","why":"The German flash leads the euro area print by a day"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area flash HICP, November (Trading Economics calendar; earlier listed here for 2 Dec; Pantheon 4.1%); final manufacturing PMI; euro area unemployment for October follows the same week","why":"The last inflation print before the 16-17 Dec ECB meeting; unemployment is 6.5% after upward revisions"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area final manufacturing PMI, November (flash 52.9)","why":"Confirms or revises the two-month high"},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Euro area final composite and services PMIs, November; euro area unemployment, October (prior 6.5%); euro area producer prices, October (prior +11.1% y/y)","why":"Final confirmation of the November flash and the last labour print before the ECB meeting"},{"date":"2026-12-07","event":"German factory orders, October (prior +4.4% m/m, +3.3% y/y); German industrial production follows on 8 Dec","why":"The German swing economy after the September rebound"},{"date":"2026-12-09","event":"Fed decision (8-9 Dec; CME 97.8% priced for 25 bp after core PCE 3.3%, with a second hike about 63% priced for January)","why":"Sets the dollar leg of EUR/USD below 1.10 and the global curve the Bund follows"},{"date":"2026-12-11","event":"Lecornu’s follow-up meeting with the civil-service unions after the professional elections, with first pay measures promised by mid-December","why":"Decides whether the 17 Nov strike becomes a December movement during the finance bill"},{"date":"2026-12-14","event":"Euro area industrial production, October (prior +0.2% m/m, −1.5% y/y)","why":"Whether the September monthly gain holds against a −1.5% annual rate"},{"date":"2026-12-16","event":"UK CPI, November (prior 3.6%, core 2.8%, services 3.7%); UK labour market report","why":"The last UK inflation print before the 17 Dec BoE decision, with December about 80% priced"},{"date":"2026-12-17","event":"ECB decision and projections (16-17 Dec; LSEG 87% priced for 25 bp to 2.75% on 25 Nov, Kocher expects the inflation projections revised up, at least two more priced for 2027); BoE decision (about 80% priced, at least three more by end-2027); French constitutional 70-day limit for the finance bill","why":"The December hike is the base case for both; the projections set the 2027 path and the second hike"},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"ECB wage tracker update; BoJ decision (79% priced for a hike)","why":"The tracker is the main evidence against a wage-price loop; the BoJ leg sets USD/JPY after the yen’s safe-haven bid"}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"The ECB held at 2.50% on 29 Oct and a 25 bp hike to 2.75% on 17 Dec is the base case: LSEG pricing was 87% on 25 Nov (Reuters), Kocher said the ECB is likely to revise its inflation outlook up in December, that demand is not driving prices, that second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027 and that market pricing is not completely unreasonable, and Trading Economics cites two more hikes priced by March 2027. The 25 Nov non-policy meeting postponed any decision on doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% until after the December meeting, possibly to spring 2027 (Handelsblatt), with Wunsch and Schnabel opposed and Simkus in favour. The account of the October meeting is out on 26 Nov. The BoE is at 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike and Greene backed another rise on 24 Nov. The Fed is 97.8% priced for a hike on 9 Dec after core PCE rose to 3.3%."},"cbName":"ECB stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The STOXX 600 fell 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov, banks about −3%, the Euro Stoxx 50 at a May low, the VIX closed 28.29 after topping 30 and the S&P 500 fell 3.2% intraday","b":"Wall Street recovered to close the S&P 500 −0.25% and the Dow +0.33% with 370 S&P advancers against 128 decliners, and the STOXX 600 held 0.4 points above its 20 Nov low","read":"US breadth improved while the AI complex kept falling, and Europe’s sell-off is now in banks and exporters rather than only chips. Under the framework the posture stays Risk-off until both restore conditions hold at a close; a STOXX 600 close under 625.65 would deepen it.","trigger":"A STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25 restores Neutral; a BTP spread above 150 bp, an OAT spread above 180 bp or iTraxx Crossover above 400 bp makes the Risk-off a credit one."},{"a":"The Ifo is 90.6, the highest since May 2023, Germany’s flash composite PMI is 55.0 and euro area new orders rose for a fifth month","b":"Destatis revised German Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q with exports −1.0%, equipment investment −3.5% and manufacturing value added −0.3%, France’s composite PMI is 46.2 and euro area industrial production is −1.5% y/y","read":"Sentiment and hard data disagree. The framework reads leading data first, so the regime stays Slowdown rather than Contraction, but it does not reopen Expansion until the composite PMI holds above 52 and the hard data turn.","trigger":"A composite final PMI above 52 on 3 Dec with EC sentiment above 97 on 27 Nov reopens Expansion; a composite under 51 or a negative Q4 German print deepens the Slowdown."},{"a":"Kocher says the December inflation projections will likely be revised up, second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027, LSEG prices an 87% December hike and two more by March 2027, and Brent rose 3.5% on the Ras Tanura attack","b":"The Bund fell to 3.22%, a three-month low, the 2-year to about 3.03%, TTF fell 4.7% to €87.33, flash PMI selling prices rose at the weakest pace since August and German growth was revised down","read":"The bond market is pricing a short cycle that ends near 3%, not a 2027 extension. The curve is betting the growth hit and the gas pullback do the ECB’s work; the Council is betting on wages and energy pass-through.","trigger":"A November flash HICP above 4.0% on 1 Dec or TTF back above €95 keeps 2027 hikes priced; a core print at or below 2.5% and TTF under €80 price them out."},{"a":"The ECB postponed the reserve-ratio increase past December and the OAT eased to 4.74%, the lowest since 9 Nov, with the OAT-Bund spread about 152 bp","b":"Banks still fell about 3% on 25 Nov, the BTP spread widened to 111.9 bp, Fitch set the Western European sovereign outlook at deteriorating and the Senate still has to pass the revenue part of the budget on 28 Nov","read":"The deferral removes a near-term liquidity drain but did not help bank equity, which is trading rates and the equity sell-off. Sovereign spreads are calm under the stress lines; the French vote and Spain’s election on 29 Nov are the next political tests.","trigger":"An OAT-Bund spread above 180 bp or a BTP spread above 150 bp at a close turns the Risk-off into a sovereign one; a Senate pass on 28 Nov and a clear Spanish majority keep spreads range-bound."},{"a":"TTF fell 4.73% to €87.33 on milder forecasts and higher Norwegian flows, and the IRGC’s ship seizure near Hormuz did not lift gas","b":"Brent settled $94.61 (+3.5%) after Houthi attacks on Ras Tanura, Jizan and Jeddah, storage is below 69% against an 86% to 89% average and Qatari LNG remains disrupted","read":"Gas is trading weather and Norway while oil trades the Gulf and the Red Sea. For Europe the gas channel matters more for HICP and the regime; the oil channel matters more for the ECB’s projections and the euro.","trigger":"TTF under €80 at a close removes the bad flag; above €95 or a confirmed hit to Ras Tanura exports puts both energy channels on bad."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The sell-off broadened from chips to banks; the posture stays Risk-off with the restore line 14 points away.","src":"Reuters via Longbridge; ad-hoc-news; dpa-AFX via ariva; finanzen.at; Cappnotes","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −1.06% to 626.03, a third close under the 635 trigger and 0.4 points above the 20 Nov low: banks about −3% (UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%, Commerzbank −3.9%), tech down for the ninth day in ten (ASM International −7.8%, Infineon −6.5%, ASML −5.8%, STMicro −4.7%), Prosus −4.9%, Rockwool −15.6%; autos +1.5% on the EU hybrid-import cap (VW +4.6%, BMW +3.7%), Remy Cointreau +13.1%, Bureau Veritas +6.6%, Sanofi +4.9%. Euro Stoxx 50 −1.82% to 5,894.46 (May low), DAX −1.26% to 24,702.13, CAC 40 −0.8%, IBEX −2.0%, FTSE 100 −0.38% to 10,426.94. S&P 500 closed −0.25% at 7,182.78 after −3.2% intraday, Nasdaq −0.62%, Dow +0.33%, VIX 28.29.","url":"https://longbridge.com/en/news/306829837"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The rate matches the existing ceiling, so the direct hit is limited, but the stacking question and the EU’s rejection reopen the trade risk into 2027.","src":"The Hill (Politico EU quotes); LaPresse; Al Jazeera/Reuters; netz-trends (deadlines); Cappnotes","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR Section 301 determinations on 14 partners: proposed 15% on EU goods net of MFN and existing duties, 20% on China, 15% on Japan; comments due 5 Jan 2027, hearing from 25 Jan. Commission spokesperson Olof Gill: the EU strongly rejects the premise that Europe is a source of structural overcapacity and expects the 15% all-in ceiling of the July 2025 joint statement to be respected; Bernd Lange calls the findings absurd. Beijing calls overcapacity a false issue.","url":"https://thehill.com/policy/international/6244220-eu-rejects-us-tariff-proposal/amp/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The liquidity drain is deferred but the December hike is firm and the projections will likely move up; bonds rallied on the equity sell-off, not on the ECB.","src":"Democrata/Handelsblatt; Newsquawk and Global Eye (Kocher); Reuters via Longbridge (LSEG); Trading Economics; ANSA","tag":"Policy","title":"ECB non-policy meeting postpones the decision on doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% until after the December meeting and possibly to spring 2027 (Handelsblatt): Wunsch calls it a tax, Schnabel is opposed, Simkus in favour. Kocher: the ECB is likely to revise its inflation outlook up in December, demand is not driving prices, second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027, market pricing is not completely unreasonable. LSEG prices 87% for a December hike. Bund 3.22%, a three-month low; BTP spread 111.9 bp.","url":"https://www.democrata.es/en/economy/the-ecb-delays-deciding-on-raising-the-minimum-bank-reserves/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"German hard data lag the 90.6 Ifo and 55.0 PMI, keeping the regime in Slowdown; the hot US print firms the Fed leg and the dollar.","src":"Destatis PD 409; Boursorama/Reuters (INSEE); Cappnotes (BEA, CME); netz-trends","tag":"Data","title":"Destatis revises German Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q from +0.2%: exports −1.0%, imports −1.4%, equipment investment −3.5%, construction −0.3%, private consumption +0.1%, manufacturing value added −0.3%; +1.1% y/y calendar-adjusted. INSEE French consumer confidence 82 in November from a revised 81 (82 expected). US October core PCE 3.3% y/y (3.2% expected, +0.3% m/m), headline 3.8%; Q3 GDP revised to 4.3% annualised from 3.4%; CME prices a 9 Dec Fed hike at 97.8%.","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2026/11/PD26_409_811.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Gas eased but stays above the €80 bad line while the oil risk premium returned through the Red Sea and Saudi infrastructure.","src":"Trading Economics; Tradingpedia/ICE; The Nation/Reuters; The Private Banker; PiQ (Fitch)","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF −4.73% to €87.33 on milder overnight forecasts and higher Norwegian flows after four gains, with storage below 69% and Gulf LNG still disrupted; the IRGC stopped a container ship near Hormuz on 24 Nov. Brent settled $94.61 (+3.5%) and WTI $83.68 after the Houthis claimed an attack on Saudi Arabia’s Ras Tanura refinery and Riyadh’s financial district; Saudi Arabia downed four drones over Ras Tanura, Jizan port was hit and a drone was destroyed over Jeddah. OPEC+ meets 29 Nov. Fitch (24 Nov) sets the 2027 outlook for Western European sovereign ratings at deteriorating.","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/news/world/40073934"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday’s rebound was short-covering; the posture stays Risk-off with the index 7 points below the 640 restore line.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −0.66% to 632.74, back under the 635 trigger, as the chip rally fades: Aixtron −16.3% (UBS sell), STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASML −5.6%; miners almost −5% (Aurubis −15%, Antofagasta −8.1%); Sanofi +9.8% on its $41B Insmed deal, health care +2%. Euro Stoxx 50 −1.16% to 6,003.76, DAX −1.31% to 25,005.51, CAC 40 −0.05% to 8,040.49, FTSE 100 −0.13% to 10,467.07, IBEX −0.86%, FTSE MIB −1.84%.","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/european-shares-end-lower-as-tech-rally-fades-sanofi-gains-ce7851dbdd89f025"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Backs Germany’s 55.0 flash PMI and the ECB hawks, but the manufacturing setback and narrow base keep the regime in Slowdown.","src":"ActionForex; Reuters via RTE; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Data","title":"Ifo business climate 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 (90.5 expected), the highest since May 2023; current conditions 89.4, expectations 91.9; services +2.4, manufacturing −1.2 from +0.9. Fuest: recovery proceeding but lacks breadth. US Conference Board confidence 103.3 from 91.9 (92.2 expected).","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Keeps 17 Dec at 2.75% as the base case for the ECB and a further BoE hike in play.","src":"Reuters via Global Banking & Finance; Newsquawk; TMGM","tag":"Policy","title":"Sleijpen: a December ECB hike is likely, the direction is clear, the path after is unclear; France’s finances must be fixed in Paris. Simkus: more tightening needed in 2027 if inflation persists. BoE’s Greene backs another rate rise; 50 bp would need clear evidence of energy costs feeding wages and prices.","url":"https://www.globalbankingandfinance.com/ecb-policy-direction-clear-december-uncertain-sleijpen/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"A new trade risk for German exporters just as the Ifo improves; no rate or date confirmed.","src":"finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Trade","title":"Reports that Washington is preparing higher tariffs on imports from the EU, China and other countries over state subsidies (dpa-AFX); Reuters says new tariffs on 16 trading partners could be announced. DHL −3.1%, Kühne+Nagel −3.6%. S&P 500 −0.50% to 7,201.09, Nasdaq −1.48%, Dow +0.85% to 50,018.70, Nvidia −5.86%.","url":"https://www.finanzen.net/nachricht/aktien/roundup-aktien-europa-schluss-eurostoxx-schwach-rueckschlag-fuer-techwerte-16084557"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Gas stays above the €80 bad line, keeping the inflation score near its low.","src":"Trading Economics; Newsquawk; ANSA","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF +1.69% to €91.25 on cold into early December, weak wind and storage below 69%. Iran’s army chief Hatami orders Hormuz kept closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met; Qatar’s PM says Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks. Bund 3.26%, BTP-Bund 109.4 bp, EUR/USD +0.53% to 1.0961. FT: US delays about 60 Patriot interceptors for Ukraine.","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"A relief rally inside the Risk-off: the STOXX 600 is 3 points short of the 640 restore line and the VIX closed 26.63, above 25.","src":"Trading Economics; Anadolu; Reuters via MarketScreener; Saxo; finanzen.net","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 +1.81% to 636.97, its best day since early May after five straight losses, as Nvidia reinstates Jensen Huang: technology +4.7% (ASML +8.92%, STMicro +8.99%, BE Semi +9.2%, AT&S +13%, Infineon +6.56%), Siemens Energy +6.88%, Deutsche Bank +4.79%, HSBC +3.2%; Nemetschek −5.7% on a JPMorgan downgrade. Euro Stoxx 50 +2.23% to 6,074.47, DAX +2.15% to 25,337.27, CAC 40 +1.48% to 8,044.84, FTSE 100 +1.30% to 10,480.82, IBEX +1.63% to 19,181.10, FTSE MIB +0.65% to 49,021.93.","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/stock-market"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The US leg of the Risk-off reversed on a governance decision, not on data; the VIX stayed above the 25 restore line.","src":"Saxo Asia Quick Take; Anadolu; KuCoin; Business Recorder (Reuters)","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 +4.73% to 7,237.17, its best day since April 2025 and the end of a seven-day fall; Nasdaq +6.51% to 24,089.56, Nasdaq-100 +6.9%, Dow +3.01% to 49,595.31; Nvidia +31.5%, a record $952B one-day gain, Philadelphia semis +14.03%; VIX −25.8% to 26.63; 2-year Treasury 4.696%, 10-year 4.948%; Brent −3.5% to $89.37, WTI −4.5% to $80.17; gold +2.7% to $3,634.58. Williams would not rule out consecutive hikes; Fed December about 98% priced (Saxo). US flash composite PMI 54.6 from 55.9.","url":"https://www.home.saxo/en-sg/content/articles/macro/asia-market-quick-take--24-november-2026-24112026"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The regime stays Slowdown: the composite sits between the 51 and 52 lines, with Germany and France pulling apart.","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext); ActionForex; Trading Economics","tag":"Data","title":"Euro area flash PMIs, November: composite 51.7 (unchanged, 51.9 poll), manufacturing 52.9 (52.8 poll, two-month high), services 51.2 (51.8 poll, five-month low); new orders up a fifth month, output prices weakest since August. Germany composite 55.0 (56-month high), France 46.2 (six-month low, Q4 recession risk). UK composite 52.3 from 52.9 (52.8 forecast). Williamson: near 0.3% q/q growth in Q4.","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The ECB stays Tightening with December settled and 2027 open; the hawks are arguing about pace, not direction.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; Trading Economics","tag":"Policy","title":"Simkus (Reuters, Vienna): not convinced a 25 bp December hike will be sufficient to fully control inflation; no major pressure to act quickly again after December, but further tightening might be needed in 2027 if pressures persist; plenty of early warning signs of second-round effects, wage tracker moving up slightly, low gas storage a risk. Reuters: markets see December as a done deal and one or two hikes in 2027, the first fully priced only by June; Trading Economics: two more 25 bp hikes by March 2027. Lane and Kocher also spoke.","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/december-rate-hike-may-not-be-enough-but-not-in-a-rush-to-move-again-ecbs-simkus-5044712"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"Spreads tightened on the signal, but the rule is a January idea in a minority parliament; the 28 Nov Senate vote and 17 Dec limit still bind.","src":"Reuters via BusinessDay; AFP via The Local and Malay Mail; Trading Economics; OraFinanza via Investing.com","tag":"Politics","title":"Macron tells Le Figaro that if volatility persists into January a 2011-style constitutional golden rule on public finances could be worth exploring, with parliament consulted as soon as January; it needs a three-fifths majority in joint session, Le Pen is inclined to support a version of it, the left objects. France expects a 5.4% deficit in 2026 with debt near 120% of GDP. The OAT fell 8 bp to 4.79% and the OAT-Bund spread to about 151 bp (OraFinanza 150 bp); the BTP-Bund spread closed 106 bp.","url":"https://www.businessday.co.za/world/2026-11-23-macron-revives-push-to-enshrine-fiscal-rules-in-constitution/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"Gas and oil split: TTF near €90 keeps the inflation and ECB pressure, while oil at a three-month low eases it.","src":"Geagency; Scenari Economici; Notizie Geopolitiche; Trading Economics (Brent); TVG Daily Brief; Wisevoter","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF December spikes 8.6% at the open to €96.075, a three-week high, on cold forecasts (3 to 5°C below normal this week, 5 to 8°C below in early December), low wind, Norwegian outages, storage at 69.2% on 21 Nov and Qatar’s force majeure extended to end-December, then fades to about €89.8 (+1.6%). Brent −3.5% to $89.37 and WTI −4.5% to $80.17, three-month lows, on the IEA surplus and Kpler’s 9.5 mb/d Hormuz flows (10 days to 19 Nov, 12.2 mb/d in October). Tasnim denies the Kharg convoy plan; Trump (Fox, 22 Nov) says tankers testing the blockade would be at the bottom of the sea and he is inclined to resume strikes after the New Year; Vance talks with Qatar’s PM; UKMTO reports a tanker incident near Chabahar; Houthis claim an attack on the Rabigh refinery.","url":"https://geagency.it/breaking-news/gas-prezzo-ttf-amsterdam-torna-sotto-90-euro-mwh-16/"}],"headline":"Europe fell again: the STOXX 600 lost 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov, a third close under the 635 trigger, as banks fell about 3%, chipmakers extended their slide (ASM International −7.8%, Infineon −6.5%, ASML −5.8%) and Washington proposed 15% Section 301 tariffs on EU goods, while autos rose 1.5% and Remy Cointreau 13.1%; the Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.82% to 5,894.46, a May low, the DAX 1.26% to 24,702.13 and the FTSE 100 0.38% to 10,426.94. Wall Street recovered from a 3.2% intraday drop to close the S&P 500 −0.25% with the VIX at 28.29, after core PCE rose to 3.3%. The Bund fell to 3.22%, a three-month low, the BTP spread widened to 111.9 bp, EUR/USD slipped to 1.0944, TTF fell 4.7% to €87.33 on milder forecasts while Brent settled $94.61 (+3.5%) after Houthi attacks on Ras Tanura, and German Q3 GDP was revised to +0.1% q/q. The ECB postponed the reserve-ratio decision; the regime stays Slowdown, the posture Risk-off and the ECB Tightening.","marketClose":"Wed 25 Nov 2026","notes":["Closes for 25 Nov: STOXX 600 626.03 (−1.06%) is the Reuters wire via Longbridge (verbatim ‘closed 1 per cent lower at 626.03’) and MarketScreener (login-walled, read from a summary); Cappnotes also gives −1.06%. Euro Stoxx 50 5,894.46 is ad-hoc-news and dpa-AFX via ariva. DAX 24,702.13 (−1.26%, −315.68) is finanzen.at/Dow Jones, aktiencheck/dpa-AFX and netz-trends; one finanzen.at text gives 24,733.22, not used; the 24 Nov prior is the Xetra 25,017.81 rather than the 25,005.51 shown yesterday. FTSE 100 10,426.94 is ad-hoc-news and ariva. CAC 40 (7,981), IBEX (18,636) and SMI (14,664.92) are the finanzen.at table; no FTSE MIB close was confirmed.","Bonds and FX for 25 Nov: Bund 3.22% is Trading Economics (3.2188, −4.5 bp, previous 3.26); Rallies/Reuters had 3.235% intraday; ANSA gives only the BTP (4.34%) and the 111.9 bp spread. OAT 4.738 and gilt 5.1372 are the Trading Economics tables without a timestamp, so the OAT-Bund spread of about 152 bp is derived. EUR/USD 1.0944 is the Trading Economics dated close (ECB reference rate 1.0935; FinScans 1.0948 intraday); Trading Economics implies a 1.0966 prior against the 1.0961 recorded yesterday.","TTF €87.33 is the Trading Economics 25 Nov value (previous 91.67, which differs from the €91.25 recorded for 24 Nov; Tradingpedia/ICE 87.80 at 11:40 GMT); no exchange settlement page was opened. Brent $94.61 and WTI $83.68 are Reuters settlements via The Nation and The Private Banker.","US: S&P 500 7,182.78, Nasdaq 23,585.80, Dow 50,182.95, VIX 28.29, Nvidia $149.30, 10-year 4.85% and DXY 103.71 are Cappnotes (AI-assisted recap citing CNBC and Yahoo) and The Company Chronicle; PCE, GDP and CME 97.8% are the same pages. Tariff rates by partner (EU 15%, China 20%, Japan 15%) are Cappnotes and netz-trends; The Hill and Al Jazeera confirm 14 partners and the EU 15%; the USTR release itself was not opened.","Policy: the reserve-ratio postponement is Handelsblatt via Democrata (wire copy); Kocher’s remarks are Newsquawk’s analysis and a Global Eye page marked unconfirmed citing FirstSquawk, with no transcript opened. The 87% December probability is LSEG via the Reuters wire on Longbridge. German GDP components are the Destatis release text; INSEE 82 is Boursorama/Reuters. The Italian 25 Nov auction results and the Trump-Rutte outcome were not found on any page read.","Score changes this run: growth −1 to 45 on the German GDP revision; markets −2 to 26 on the STOXX 600 at 626.03, the VIX at 28.29, banks −3% and the wider BTP spread; inflation and policy unchanged; composite 32. No score moved 5 or more."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Stays Risk-off. The STOXX 600 fell 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov, a third day under the 635 trigger and 14 points short of the 640 restore line, as banks fell about 3% on the ECB reserve-ratio debate and rate fears, technology fell for the ninth day in ten (ASML −5.8%, ASM International −7.8%, Infineon −6.5%) and US tariff determinations hit exporters, while autos rose 1.5%; the Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.82% to 5,894.46, its lowest since May, the DAX 1.26% to 24,702.13 and the IBEX 2.0%. In the US the S&P 500 closed −0.25% at 7,182.78 after a 3.2% intraday fall, the Nasdaq −0.62% after −4.6% intraday, and the VIX closed 28.29 after topping 30, above the 25 restore line. Sovereigns stayed calm: the BTP-Bund spread 111.9 bp and the OAT-Bund spread about 152 bp, under the 150/180 bp lines that would make this a sovereign Risk-off. Neutral returns on a STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25."},"regime":{"label":"Slowdown","note":"Stays Slowdown. Destatis revised German Q3 GDP down to +0.1% q/q from the +0.2% flash on 25 Nov, with exports −1.0%, equipment investment −3.5% and private consumption +0.1%, so the 90.6 Ifo and the 55.0 flash composite PMI are running ahead of the hard data. INSEE consumer confidence rose to 82 in November from a revised 81 (82 expected), still under its 100 average. The euro area flash composite PMI is 51.7, between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion, with France at 46.2. Euro area hard data remain weak: September industrial production −1.5% y/y, retail sales +0.4% y/y, unemployment 6.5%, Q3 GDP +0.4% q/q. EC sentiment on 27 Nov, flash HICP on 1 Dec and the final PMIs on 1 and 3 Dec are next."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":48.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":50.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.2},{"l":"May 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":52.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":52.9}],"latest":"52.9","name":"HCOB Eurozone Manufacturing PMI","next":"Early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"52.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash rose to 52.9 from 52.7, a two-month high and above the 52.8 Reuters poll, the tenth month above 50; the output index rose to 53.3 from 52.8. Total new orders across the economy rose for a fifth month at a slightly quicker pace and manufacturers’ order growth was stronger than services’, while new export orders grew at the slowest pace in three months; factory hiring was at a near three-and-a-half-year high. Input-cost inflation eased to a three-month low and selling prices rose at the weakest pace since August. Germany rose to 55.0 from 54.2 with output at 57.4, a 63-month high; France fell to 47.9 from 49.5 with output at 44.5, the weakest in 21 months. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, beat poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Two-month high","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"−0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":52.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":47.6},{"l":"May 26","v":47.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":49.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.2}],"latest":"51.2","name":"HCOB Eurozone Services PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.4 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash fell to 51.2 from 51.4, a five-month low and below the 51.8 Reuters poll that had looked for a rise. Services cut jobs for a second month, the year-ahead outlook fell for a fourth month to its lowest since May and output-price inflation slowed. Germany’s services PMI rose to 54.0 from 52.5; France’s rose to 46.6 from 46.2 but stayed in contraction with new business down markedly, and S&P Global’s Hayes said the French data raise the odds of a Q4 technical recession. The rest of the euro area grew at its slowest pace in six months; Finimize’s read of Williamson is that services feel high living costs and political uncertainty while investment orders hold up.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, missed poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Five-month low","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"0.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":48.8},{"l":"May 26","v":48.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":50},{"l":"Jul 26","v":52},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.7}],"latest":"51.7","name":"HCOB Eurozone Composite PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash composite held at 51.7, unchanged from October and below the 51.9 Reuters poll, a fifth straight month above 50; it sits between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion. Manufacturing (52.9) carried the index while services (51.2) slipped. New orders rose for a fifth month and slightly faster, employment was broadly unchanged, input costs rose at a three-month low pace and output prices at the weakest since August. Germany’s composite rose to 55.0 from 53.4, a 56-month high; France fell to 46.2 from 46.6, its sharpest contraction in six months with sentiment near its worst since 2012 outside Covid. Williamson: steady growth consistent with a near 0.3% q/q pace in Q4, masking worrying divergences.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, below 52","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics (23 Nov)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Unchanged","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"+1.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":-13.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-12.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-12.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-16.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-20.5},{"l":"May 26","v":-18.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-17.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-15.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-15.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-16.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":-17.9},{"l":"Nov 26","v":-16.9}],"latest":"−16.9","name":"Euro Area Consumer Confidence","next":"27 Nov (final, with ESI)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"−17.9","read":"The November flash rose to −16.9 from −17.9 in October, one point better than the −17.9 consensus and the first improvement since August, but still below the −15 weak line of the framework and about six points under the long-run average of −11. The final October economic sentiment indicator was 96.9, a four-month low; the November ESI and the final confidence figure print on 27 Nov. Consumers remain the weak leg of the leading set, with German business surveys holding up and the French INSEE climate at a June low.","ref":-11,"refLabel":"−11 = long-run average","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Weak, below −15","src":"European Commission flash (20 Nov) via Newsquawk and fx.co; Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"+7.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":45.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":59.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-0.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-17.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-10.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":26.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":34.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":34.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":28.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":35.6}],"latest":"35.6","name":"ZEW Economic Sentiment, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"28.1","read":"Rose to 35.6 in November from 28.1, above the 28.3 consensus (Newsquawk 29) and recovering most of October’s fall. Current conditions improved to −33.2 from −35.9, in line with consensus, and euro area expectations rose to 26.5 from 18.5 against 18.3, with the euro area current index at −14.7. Wambach: the German economy remains on its recovery track at a slow rate, supported by rising manufacturing orders and lower oil prices, but government spending has been the main driver so far, which raises debt-sustainability concerns. One beat does not change the regime; the 23 Nov flash PMIs and 24 Nov Ifo follow.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = neutral","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above consensus","src":"ZEW via ActionForex and Newsquawk (17 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661152-germany-zew-sentiment-jumps-to-35-6-but-recovery-remains-uneven/"},{"change":"+0.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":87.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":87.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":88.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":86.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":84.4},{"l":"May 26","v":84.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":85.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":86.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":88.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":89.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":90.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":90.6}],"latest":"90.6","name":"Ifo Business Climate, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec (December)","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"90.1 (Oct, rev. from 90.2)","read":"The Ifo rose to 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 against 90.5 expected (Reuters poll), a seventh straight rise and the highest since May 2023. Current conditions rose to 89.4 from 88.8 and expectations to 91.9 from 91.5 (ActionForex and Trading Economics; Newsquawk and QNC print 91.6), slightly under the 92.0 consensus; services rose to +2.4 from −1.2 while manufacturing fell back to −1.2 from +0.9, its first setback after six gains, with expectations negative again. Fuest said the recovery is proceeding but lacks breadth and is mostly driven by deficit-financed public spending; the reading backs the 55.0 German flash PMI but not a broad-based upturn.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since May 2023","src":"Ifo Institute via ActionForex (sector balances), Reuters via RTE (poll 90.5, Fuest), Trading Economics, Newsquawk","trend":"up","trendWord":"Seventh rise","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−0.4 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-1.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-1.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-1.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-1.5}],"latest":"−1.5%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"14 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"−1.1%","read":"Rose 0.2% on the month in September, the first monthly gain in four months, but fell 1.5% on the year from −1.1% in August (August −0.5% m/m and −1.1% y/y, unrevised). Durable consumer goods rose 1.3% m/m and intermediate goods 0.7%, capital goods were flat, energy fell 1.7% and non-durable consumer goods 0.4%; the EU rose 0.5% m/m and fell 0.8% y/y, with Ireland, Bulgaria and Romania the largest annual falls and the Netherlands, Hungary, Lithuania and Malta the largest rises. The annual rate is the weakest of the year and sits against a manufacturing PMI of 52.7, so the coincident set still does not confirm the survey; ING’s Colijn said signs of a true rebound are not there. The Eurostat page redirected, so the figures are the Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion reports of the release.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Fourth annual fall, m/m up","src":"Eurostat (4-16112026-AP, 16 Nov 2026) via Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-16112026-ap"},{"change":"−0.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1},{"l":"May 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.3% (rev. from +0.8%)","read":"Volumes fell 0.7% on the month in September against a −0.1% consensus, the sharpest fall in over a year, and rose 0.4% on the year after August was revised up to +0.4% m/m and +1.3% y/y. Non-food fell 1.2% m/m, food 0.3% and fuel rose 1.0%; Germany fell 2.0% m/m and 2.4% y/y, France 0.6% m/m (+2.8% y/y), Spain 0.4% and Italy rose 0.1%. Consumers are cutting real spending as HICP runs at 4.0% and confidence sits at −17.9; the annual rate is still positive, so the signal is warn rather than bad.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Sharp monthly fall","src":"Eurostat (6 Nov 2026 release)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/en/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-06112026-ap"},{"change":"0.0 pt (+0.1 pt vs first print)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.4},{"l":"May 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.5}],"latest":"6.5%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"6.5% (rev. from 6.4%)","read":"September unemployment printed 6.5% against 6.4% expected, and the Eurostat series now shows July and August at 6.5% too (revised from 6.4%), so the rate has risen 0.2 pt from the 6.3% low of January. 11.555M people are unemployed, up 122K on the month, youth unemployment rose to 15.4% from 15.1%; Spain 10.3%, France 8.5%, Italy 6.3%, Germany 4.1%, Netherlands 4.0%; the EU rate is 6.2%. The level is still low in the five-year range but the direction has turned.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Rising from 6.3% low","src":"Eurostat API (une_rt_m, updated 30 Oct); Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/unemployment-rate"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":10.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":13},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.6},{"l":"May 26","v":6.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":13.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.3}],"latest":"+3.3%","name":"German Factory Orders, y/y","next":"7 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+3.2% (rev. from +2.7%)","read":"Orders rose 4.4% on the month in September against a 4.8% consensus, recovering part of the August fall, which was revised to −10.1% from −10.6%. Orders are 3.3% above a year ago, and the August annual rate was revised to +3.2% from +2.7%. The rebound was broad: +5.1% excluding large orders, domestic +6.6%, foreign +2.8% (euro area +4.5%), autos +10.5% and machinery +8.3%, while other transport equipment fell 10.4%. Q3 orders were only 0.2% above Q2 and real manufacturing turnover rose 0.2% on the month, so the level is flat, not accelerating.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Broad rebound","src":"Destatis (press release 385, 5 Nov 2026); Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound m/m","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2026/11/PD26_385_421.html"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q4 23","v":0},{"l":"Q1 24","v":0.4},{"l":"Q2 24","v":0.2},{"l":"Q3 24","v":0.5},{"l":"Q4 24","v":0.3},{"l":"Q1 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Q2 25","v":0},{"l":"Q3 25","v":0.3},{"l":"Q4 25","v":0.2},{"l":"Q1 26","v":0},{"l":"Q2 26","v":0.6},{"l":"Q3 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Real GDP, q/q","next":"Early Dec (Q3 third estimate)","period":"Q3 2026 second estimate","prior":"+0.6%","read":"The second estimate on 13 Nov confirmed Q3 growth at +0.4% q/q (consensus 0.4%) and +1.3% y/y after 0.6% in Q2, with employment stable on the quarter: Ireland +5.5%, Spain +0.6%, Germany +0.2%, Italy 0.0%, France −0.1%; the EU grew 0.5%. Growth is positive, so the signal is good, but the composition is AI investment, defence and public spending with France and Italy flat to negative, the September goods balance swung to a €10.3B deficit on energy imports, and the ECB expects the yield rise to cost about 100 bp of growth over 2027-28. UK Q3 GDP rose 0.7% q/q the day before.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"good","signalWord":"Confirmed +0.4%","src":"Eurostat (2-13112026-AP, 13 Nov) via investingLive, Newsquawk and Eunews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, beat","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/2-13112026-ap"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4}],"latest":"4.0%","name":"HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"3.8%","read":"Eurostat confirmed October HICP at 4.0% y/y on 18 Nov, up from 3.8% and the highest since September 2023. Energy was 21.0% (19.0% in September; flash 20.8%), services 3.3% (3.2%), non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%) and food, alcohol and tobacco 1.5%, revised from 1.6% in the flash (1.3% in September). Lithuania printed 6.1%, Bulgaria 5.9%, Greece 5.7% and Italy 5.6%, up 1.4 pt on the month; Germany’s final was 2.9%. Pantheon projects 4.1% in November, 4.3% in December and a 4.5% peak in January, and the ECB expects inflation above 4% for much of the first half of 2027. The November flash is due 1 Dec. The Eurostat release page redirected to a maintenance notice; the figures are from Eunews and Trading Economics.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Confirmed, highest since Sep 2023","src":"Eurostat (2-18112026-AP, 18 Nov) via Eunews and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.eunews.it/en/2026/11/18/eurozone-inflation-confirmed-at-4-per-cent-in-october/"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.2},{"l":"May 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6}],"latest":"2.6%","name":"Core HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"2.5%","read":"Core HICP was revised up to 2.6% in the 18 Nov final from the 2.5% flash, a five-month high and the second monthly rise, with services at 3.3% (3.2% in September) and non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%). The energy shock is starting to show in underlying prices, though the ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov still has negotiated wages at 2.3% for 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027. Lagarde said underlying inflation would take a particular role in the December decision; a core print above 2.6% in the 1 Dec flash strengthens the case for the market’s second 2027 hike. The Q3 negotiated wages print is 20 Nov.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Five-month high","src":"Eurostat (18 Nov final) via Trading Economics; ECB wage tracker (4 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.5},{"l":"25 Nov","v":2.5}],"latest":"2.50%","name":"ECB Deposit Facility Rate","next":"16–17 Dec meeting (projection round)","period":"Since 16 Sep; held 29 Oct","prior":"2.25%","read":"The deposit rate is 2.50% after hikes in June and September and a hold on 29 Oct; a 25 bp hike to 2.75% on 17 Dec is the base case (LSEG 87% on 25 Nov per Reuters; Trading Economics cites two more hikes priced by March 2027). Kocher said on 25 Nov that the ECB is likely to revise its inflation outlook up in December, that demand is not driving prices, that second-round effects become clearer in H1 2027 and that market pricing is not completely unreasonable; Sleijpen called a December hike likely on 24 Nov with the path after unclear. The 25 Nov non-policy meeting postponed any decision on doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% until after the December meeting and possibly to spring 2027 (Handelsblatt via Democrata), with Wunsch calling it a tax, Schnabel opposed and Simkus in favour. The account of the October meeting is published 26 Nov and the European Council begins consultations on the presidency the same day.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening","src":"ECB key rates; Reuters via Longbridge (LSEG 87%); Newsquawk and Global Eye (Kocher); Democrata/Handelsblatt (reserve ratio postponed); Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/key_ecb_interest_rates/html/index.en.html"},{"change":"+0.12 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q3 25","v":1.89},{"l":"Q4 25","v":2.95},{"l":"Q1 26","v":2.56},{"l":"Q2 26","v":2.45},{"l":"Q3 26","v":2.57}],"latest":"2.57%","name":"Negotiated Wages, y/y","next":"Feb 2027 (Q4); tracker 18 Dec","period":"Q3 2026","prior":"2.45% (rev. from 2.44%)","read":"Negotiated wages rose 2.57% y/y in Q3 2026 from a revised 2.45% in Q2, a modest acceleration of 0.12 point and far below the 5.55% peak of 2024, so the 4.0% HICP print has not yet fed into pay settlements. The ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov shows 2.7% for the second half of 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027 with smoothed one-offs, on 45.6% and 31.8% coverage; the September staff projection has compensation per employee at 3.3% for 2026. This remains the main argument that the energy shock is not turning into a wage-price loop, and Nagel said on 19 Nov he sees no spiral. The next tracker update is 18 Dec, after the ECB meeting.","ref":3,"refLabel":"3% = roughly consistent with 2% inflation","signal":"good","signalWord":"No wage spiral","src":"ECB indicator of negotiated wages (20 Nov) via Poland Insight; ECB wage tracker release of 4 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/negotiated-wage-growth"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.8},{"l":"May 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.6}],"latest":"3.6%","name":"UK CPI, y/y","next":"16 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"3.5%","read":"Rose to 3.6% in October from 3.5%, a one-year high but below the 3.7% consensus and the BoE’s 3.7% projection. Core held at 2.8%, services eased to 3.7% (consensus 3.8%, BoE 3.9%), goods rose to 3.5% from 3.2% and CPIH to 3.7%; prices rose 0.5% on the month. Transport, mainly motor fuels, was the largest upward contribution with the earlier tobacco duty rise, restaurants and hotels the main offset; food rose 0.9%, the smallest since September 2021. The BoE projects nearly 5% in Q2 2027 on energy. December hike pricing fell to about 80% from 90% with three more hikes by June still priced; the 10-year gilt fell 9 bp to 5.30%. Next release 16 Dec.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoE target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"One-year high, below consensus","src":"ONS (18 Nov 2026 bulletin); Reuters via Investing.com; Trading Economics (gilts and pricing); Alliance News via BritBrief","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/bulletins/consumerpriceinflation/october2026"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.75},{"l":"May 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.75},{"l":"25 Nov","v":4}],"latest":"4.00%","name":"BoE Bank Rate","next":"17 Dec","period":"Since 5 Nov 2026","prior":"3.75%","read":"Bank Rate is 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike, the first since 2023, by 8 votes to 1. October CPI of 3.6% (3.7% expected) cut December pricing to about 80%, and the 20 Nov data were firm: retail sales rose 0.7% m/m (flat expected, ex-fuel +0.9%, +2.3% y/y), GfK confidence improved to −22 from −24 and October borrowing was £17.0B against £18.0B expected and a revised £22.9B in September. Trading Economics says markets price at least three more hikes by the end of 2027. The 10-year gilt fell below 5.3% to 5.24% on global risk aversion as the FTSE 100 lost 1.02% and 5.1% on the week; sterling ended about 1.31. The UK-EU Brussels summit agreed to link emissions trading systems, reduce agri-food checks from 2028 and start an economic-security dialogue. Greene told a SIGNUM roundtable on 24 Nov that she backs another rate increase, that disinflation has stalled and that a 50 bp move would need clear evidence of energy costs passing into wages and prices (TMGM summary).","signal":"warn","signalWord":"First hike since 2023","src":"Bank of England (Monetary Policy Summary, 5 Nov 2026); ONS retail sales and public finances via MT Newswires and Share Talk (20 Nov); Trading Economics gilt news (20 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"−4.5 bp (TE 3.2188)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.81},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.91},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.05},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.96},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.07},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.58},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.32},{"l":"25 Nov","v":3.22}],"latest":"3.22%","name":"10-Year Bund Yield","period":"25 Nov","prior":"3.26% on 24 Nov","read":"The Bund fell to 3.22% on 25 Nov (Trading Economics 3.2188, −4.5 bp from 3.26; ANSA’s close implies 3.22 from the 4.34% BTP and 111.9 bp spread; Rallies/Reuters had 3.235% intraday), its lowest since late August, as technology losses sent money into bonds and Germany sold €5B of 10-year paper in its final benchmark sale of the year. The Bund future rose 39 ticks to 123.43. The 2-year fell 4 bp to about 3.03%. The rally came despite Kocher saying the December projections will likely raise the inflation path and an 87% December hike probability, so the curve is pricing a short cycle: the 10-year is 36 bp under its September high of 3.58%.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Above 3%","src":"Trading Economics (3.2188, −4.5 bp, three-month low); ANSA (BTP 4.34%, spread 111.9 bp); Rallies.ai/Reuters (3.235% intraday, €5B auction); finanzen.at (Bund future 123.43)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Three-month low","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/germany/government-bond-yield"},{"change":"+2.5 bp (opened 109.5 bp)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":74},{"l":"Jan 26","v":68},{"l":"Feb 26","v":64},{"l":"Mar 26","v":82},{"l":"Apr 26","v":82},{"l":"May 26","v":79},{"l":"Jun 26","v":77},{"l":"Jul 26","v":81},{"l":"Aug 26","v":81},{"l":"Sep 26","v":106},{"l":"Oct 26","v":118},{"l":"25 Nov","v":111.9}],"latest":"112 bp","name":"BTP–Bund 10-Year Spread","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"109 bp on 24 Nov","read":"The spread closed 111.9 bp on 25 Nov per ANSA, from 109.4 bp, after opening at 109.5 bp, with the BTP at 4.34% from 4.35% and the Bund about 3.22%; Italian banks fell (UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%) as the ECB reserve-ratio debate and rate fears hit the sector. It stays well under the 150 bp warn line. The OAT-Bund spread is about 152 bp on the Trading Economics yields (OAT 4.738%, the lowest since 9 Nov), still at the warn line ahead of the Senate’s 28 Nov revenue vote. Fitch set its 2027 outlook for Western European sovereign ratings at deteriorating on 24 Nov. Italy sells BOTs on 26 Nov and medium and long BTPs on 27 Nov.","ref":150,"refLabel":"150 bp = warn line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Under 150 bp","src":"ANSA (open 109.5 bp, close 111.9 bp, BTP 4.34%); Trading Economics (OAT 4.738%, Bund 3.2188%); PiQ (Fitch); ad-hoc-news (bank moves)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Wider","url":"https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/topnews/2026/11/25/lo-spread-btp-bund-conclude-in-aumento-a-1119-punti_9fc83e3e-6113-44a1-9421-521e4703cb1f.html"},{"change":"−1.06% on 25 Nov","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":592.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":611},{"l":"Feb 26","v":633.85},{"l":"Mar 26","v":583.14},{"l":"Apr 26","v":611.28},{"l":"May 26","v":626},{"l":"Jun 26","v":641.73},{"l":"Jul 26","v":649.19},{"l":"Aug 26","v":651.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":634.89},{"l":"Oct 26","v":646.75},{"l":"25 Nov","v":626.03}],"latest":"626.03","name":"STOXX Europe 600","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"632.74 on 24 Nov","read":"The STOXX 600 gave up early gains and fell 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov (Reuters via Longbridge and MarketScreener; Cappnotes −1.06%), 0.4 points above the 20 Nov low of 625.65 and 5.7% below the 13 Nov record of 664.02. Banks were the worst sector at about −3% (UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%, Commerzbank −3.9%) on rate fears and the ECB reserve-ratio debate; technology fell for the ninth day in ten (ASM International −7.8%, ASML −5.8%, Infineon −6.5%, STMicro −4.7%), Prosus −4.9% on a profit miss, Rockwool −15.6% on a cut outlook, Siemens Energy −4.6%, Schneider −4.2%. Autos rose 1.5% after the EU capped Chinese hybrid imports and a JP Morgan note (VW +4.6%, BMW +3.7%); Remy Cointreau +13.1% on raised targets, Bureau Veritas +6.6% on an Advent bid report, Sanofi +4.9%, AMS-Osram +6.8% on a Samsung Display stake. USTR’s proposed 15% tariff on EU goods and hot US core PCE weighed on exporters. The index is 9 points under the 635 trigger and 14 under the 640 restore line.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Below 635 trigger","src":"Reuters via Longbridge and MarketScreener (626.03, −1%, sectors); ad-hoc-news (stock moves); dpa-AFX via ariva; finanzen.at/Dow Jones","trend":"down","trendWord":"Third day under 635","url":"https://longbridge.com/en/news/306829837"},{"change":"−1.82% on 25 Nov (−109.30)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":5791.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":5947.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6138.41},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5569.73},{"l":"Apr 26","v":5881.51},{"l":"May 26","v":6050.54},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6328.09},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6358.01},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6420.16},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6269.02},{"l":"Oct 26","v":6131.31},{"l":"25 Nov","v":5894.46}],"latest":"5,894.46","name":"Euro Stoxx 50","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"6,003.76 on 24 Nov","read":"The Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.82% to 5,894.46 on 25 Nov (ad-hoc-news and dpa-AFX via ariva), its lowest close since May and 0.8% under the 20 Nov close of 5,941.95, as banks, chips and industrials led the losses (ASML −5.8%, Infineon −6.5%, Prosus −4.9%, Schneider −4.2%, UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%) while autos rose (BMW +3.7%) and Sanofi +4.9%. The index is 4.2% lower over five sessions and about 3.9% below its October close of 6,131.31; finanzen.at puts it +3.7% year to date.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near May low","src":"ad-hoc-news (5,894.46, −1.82%); dpa-AFX via ariva (same close); finanzen.at table (−1.8%)","trend":"down","trendWord":"May low","url":"https://www.ad-hoc-news.de/boerse/news/marktberichte/euro-stoxx-50-closes-1-82-prozent-lower-at-5-894-46-as-ai-fears-return/70555310"},{"change":"−1.26% on 25 Nov (−315.68)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":24490.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":24538.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":25284.26},{"l":"Mar 26","v":22680.04},{"l":"Apr 26","v":24292.38},{"l":"May 26","v":25104.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":24995.81},{"l":"Jul 26","v":25629.24},{"l":"Aug 26","v":26258.11},{"l":"Sep 26","v":25199.19},{"l":"Oct 26","v":25818.21},{"l":"25 Nov","v":24702.13}],"latest":"24,702.13","name":"DAX","period":"25 Nov Xetra close","prior":"25,017.81 on 24 Nov (Xetra; row showed 25,005.51)","read":"The DAX fell 1.26% to 24,702.13 at the Xetra close on 25 Nov (finanzen.at/Dow Jones, aktiencheck/dpa-AFX, netz-trends; one finanzen.at text gives 24,733.22), closing near its 24,698.22 low after opening at 25,083.98, and back under 25,000 and its 200-day average. Infineon fell 6.5%, Siemens Energy 4.6%, Commerzbank 3.9%, Siemens 3.6%, Hochtief 3.4% and Bayer 2.6% on a report Kerendia will be withdrawn in Germany; VW rose 4.6%, BMW 3.7%, Heidelberg Materials 3.2%, Beiersdorf 2.2% and Schaeffler 1.0% on a JP Morgan upgrade. The MDAX fell 0.68% to 28,595.47 and the TecDAX 1.9%. Destatis revised Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q the same morning. The index is 1.0% up year to date and 8.5% below its 27,001.67 record.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Just above 25,000","src":"finanzen.at/Dow Jones (24,702.13, −315.68, −1.26%); aktiencheck/dpa-AFX (Xetra close, MDAX); netz-trends; finanzen.at (movers, range)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back under 25,000","url":"https://www.finanzen.at/nachrichten/aktien/xetra-schluss-schwach-dax-rutscht-wieder-unter-25-000-punkte-1036800435"},{"change":"−0.38% on 25 Nov (−40.13 pt)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":9931.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":10223.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":10910.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":10176.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":10378.8},{"l":"May 26","v":10409.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10497.1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":10868.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":10824.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":10606},{"l":"Oct 26","v":10729.78},{"l":"25 Nov","v":10426.94}],"latest":"10,426.94","name":"FTSE 100","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"10,467.07 on 24 Nov","read":"The FTSE 100 fell 0.38% to 10,426.94 on 25 Nov (ad-hoc-news; dpa-AFX via ariva), outperforming the continent as defensives held: Imperial Brands rose 6.1%, Unilever 2.2% and GSK 1.0%, while Computacenter fell 8.2%, Polar Capital Technology 4.9% and Scottish Mortgage 2.3% on the AI sell-off. The gilt was about 5.14% on Trading Economics. The index is 4.4% below its 13 Nov record of 10,902.56 and 2.8% below its October close; Bank Rate is 4.00% with the next BoE decision on 17 Dec.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below Oct close","src":"ad-hoc-news (10,426.94, −40.13, −0.38%, movers); dpa-AFX via ariva; Trading Economics (gilt 5.1372)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://www.ad-hoc-news.de/boerse/news/marktberichte/ftse-100-closes-0-38-prozent-lower-as-tech-turbulence-returns/70554551"},{"change":"−0.20% on 25 Nov (TE −0.0020)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1747},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.1855},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.1803},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.146},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.1685},{"l":"May 26","v":1.166},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.1422},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.1524},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.1589},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.1341},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.1184},{"l":"25 Nov","v":1.0944}],"latest":"1.0944","name":"EUR/USD","period":"25 Nov close","prior":"1.0961 on 24 Nov","read":"EUR/USD fell 0.20% to 1.0944 on 25 Nov on the Trading Economics close (ECB reference rate 1.0935; FinScans 1.0948 intraday), hovering near its weakest since March 2025 after Monday’s 1.0903, as US core PCE rose to 3.3% and Q3 GDP to 4.3% and the dollar index held near 104 (Cappnotes 103.71 at the close). Hawkish ECB remarks from Kocher and an 87% December hike probability did not help the euro while the tariff determination and the equity sell-off kept safe-haven dollar demand. The pair is 2.1% below its October close of 1.1184; a weaker euro adds to imported energy inflation.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Under 1.10","src":"Trading Economics (1.0944, −0.20%); ECB reference rate (1.0935); FinScans (1.0948, PCE and GDP); Cappnotes (DXY 103.71)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Near 20-month low","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/currency"},{"change":"−4.73% on 25 Nov (−4.34)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":31.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":28.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":28.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":39.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":46},{"l":"May 26","v":46},{"l":"Jun 26","v":43.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":59.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":69.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":72.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":103.94},{"l":"25 Nov","v":87.33}],"latest":"€87.33","name":"TTF Natural Gas","period":"25 Nov (TE close)","prior":"€91.67 on 24 Nov (TE previous; row showed €91.25)","read":"TTF front-month fell 4.73% to €87.33 per MWh on 25 Nov (Trading Economics; ICE €87.80 at 11:40 GMT per Tradingpedia), ending four days of gains as overnight forecasts turned milder and Norwegian exports to the continent were set to rise 167 GWh/day on Thursday, even though colder weather is still forecast for northwest and central Europe this week. Storage is below 69% (GIE 68.9% on 23 Nov) against a five-year seasonal average of 86% to 89%, and Gulf LNG remains disrupted with the IRGC stopping a container ship near Hormuz on 24 Nov. Oil went the other way: Brent settled $94.61, +3.5%, after the Houthis claimed an attack on Ras Tanura and Saudi Arabia downed four drones over the complex. No exchange settlement page was opened. The price is about €7 above the €80 bad line.","ref":50,"refLabel":"€50 = warn, €80 = bad","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above €80 bad line","src":"Trading Economics (87.33, previous 91.67, −4.73%); Tradingpedia/ICE (87.80 intraday, Norwegian flows); Oilprice and PiQ (storage 68.9%); The Nation/Reuters (Brent $94.61)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"},{"change":"n/a","hist":[],"latest":"≈300 bp","name":"iTraxx Crossover","period":"28 Sep 2026, intraday","prior":"About 315–320 bp in mid-May 2026","read":"No dated level was found for 17 to 23 Nov. The last confirmed print is about 300 bp touched intraday on 28 Sept 2026 (Reuters, S&P Global data), below the 315 to 320 bp range of mid-May. The row stays n/a until a dated page gives a level. The 23 Nov relief rally (STOXX 600 +1.81%, S&P 500 +4.73%, VIX down to 26.63) argues for a tighter print than Friday, while the BTP spread at 106 bp and the OAT-Bund spread near 151 bp say the sovereign market is calm.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Stale, 28 Sep","src":"Reuters via Gate (28 Sep 2026); cbonds","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.gate.com/en-us/news/detail/itraxx-european-crossover-index-touches-300-basis-points-for-first-time-17884358"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the core. Closing levels for Thursday 19 Nov unless a date is shown.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["A third day under the trigger. The STOXX 600 fell 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov (Reuters via Longbridge and MarketScreener), 0.4 points above the 20 Nov low of 625.65 and 5.7% below the 13 Nov record; banks were the worst sector at about −3% (UniCredit −3.3%, Intesa −3.3%, Commerzbank −3.9%) on rate worries and the reserve-ratio debate, technology fell for the ninth day in ten (ASM International −7.8%, ASML −5.8%, Infineon −6.5%, STMicro −4.7%), Prosus −4.9% on a profit miss, Rockwool −15.6% on a cut outlook, Siemens Energy −4.6%. Autos rose 1.5% after the EU capped Chinese hybrid imports (VW +4.6%, BMW +3.7%), Remy Cointreau +13.1%, Bureau Veritas +6.6% on bid reports, Sanofi +4.9%, AMS-Osram +6.8%. The Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.82% to 5,894.46, its lowest since May, the DAX 1.26% to 24,702.13, the CAC 40 0.8% to about 7,981, the IBEX 2.0% to about 18,636, the SMI 0.47% to 14,664.92 and the FTSE 100 0.38% to 10,426.94 (Computacenter −8.2%, Imperial Brands +6.1%).","German hard data cooled. Destatis revised Q3 GDP to +0.1% q/q from +0.2%, with exports −1.0%, imports −1.4%, equipment investment −3.5%, construction −0.3%, private consumption +0.1% and manufacturing value added −0.3%; growth is +1.1% y/y calendar-adjusted, the savings rate 9.1%. INSEE French consumer confidence rose to 82 from a revised 81, in line with consensus. Both sit against the 90.6 Ifo and the 55.0 German flash PMI, so the German upturn is a sentiment story the GDP accounts have not confirmed.","Washington formalised the tariffs. USTR made Section 301 determinations on 14 partners and proposed 15% on EU goods net of MFN and existing duties, 20% on China and 15% on Japan, with comments due 5 Jan 2027 and a hearing from 25 Jan; the Commission’s Olof Gill said the EU strongly rejects the premise that Europe is a source of structural overcapacity and that the 15% ceiling including all charges in the July 2025 joint statement must be respected, while Bernd Lange called the findings absurd. US core PCE rose to 3.3% y/y in October (3.2% expected, +0.3% m/m), headline 3.8%, and Q3 GDP was revised to 4.3% annualised from 3.4%; CME pricing for a 9 Dec Fed hike rose to 97.8%. The S&P 500 fell 3.2% intraday to 6,970.11 and closed −0.25% at 7,182.78, the Nasdaq −0.62% at 23,585.80 after −4.6%, the Dow +0.33% at 50,182.95 after a 1,105-point drop, Nvidia −3.91% to $149.30, the VIX 28.29 after topping 30; US markets are shut 26 Nov.","Bonds rallied and the euro slipped. The Bund fell to 3.22% (Trading Economics 3.2188, −4.5 bp), its lowest since late August, as Germany sold €5B of 10-year paper in its last benchmark sale of the year; the BTP closed 4.34% and the spread widened to 111.9 bp from 109.4 bp (ANSA) as Italian banks fell; the OAT eased to 4.74%, the lowest since 9 Nov, for an OAT-Bund spread of about 152 bp; the gilt was about 5.14% and the US 10-year 4.85% (−6 bp). EUR/USD fell 0.20% to 1.0944 (ECB reference 1.0935), near its weakest since March 2025, as the dollar held near 104 on the PCE print. The ECB postponed the reserve-ratio decision and Kocher said the December projections will likely lift the inflation path; LSEG prices an 87% chance of a December hike.","Energy split. TTF fell 4.73% to €87.33 (Trading Economics) on milder overnight forecasts and higher Norwegian flows for Thursday, after four gains, with storage below 69% against a five-year average of about 86% to 89% and Gulf LNG still disrupted; the IRGC stopped a container ship near Hormuz on 24 Nov. Brent settled $94.61, +3.5%, and WTI $83.68 after the Houthis claimed an attack on Saudi Arabia’s Ras Tanura refinery and Riyadh’s financial district, Saudi Arabia said it downed four drones over Ras Tanura, and Jizan and Jeddah ports were hit; OPEC+ meets 29 Nov. Fitch set North America and Western Europe sovereign outlooks at deteriorating for 2027 (24 Nov). Spain votes 29 Nov with PP plus Vox at 187 seats in the final 40dB poll; the French Senate votes the revenue part of the budget on 28 Nov."],"translation":[{"body":"The STOXX 600 fell 1.06% to 626.03 on 25 Nov, a third close under the 635 trigger and within half a point of the 20 Nov low, so the framework keeps the posture Risk-off: the VIX closed 28.29 after topping 30 and the 640 restore line is 14 points away. The selling broadened from chips to banks, which fell about 3% on rate fears and the ECB reserve-ratio debate, while autos, spirits and health care rose, so leadership is defensive and domestic rather than exporter and AI. The Euro Stoxx 50 at 5,894.46 is at a May low and 3.9% below its October close; a close under 625.65 on the STOXX 600 would deepen the Risk-off. Wall Street’s recovery from a 3.2% intraday fall and the US holiday on 26 Nov give Europe a quieter session to test that level.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"Core bonds rallied into the equity sell-off: the Bund fell to 3.22%, a three-month low, the gilt about 5.14% and the US 10-year 4.85%, even as Kocher said the December projections will likely raise the inflation path and LSEG prices an 87% December hike. The periphery widened with banks: the BTP-Bund spread closed 111.9 bp from 109.4 bp, still well under 150 bp, and the OAT-Bund spread held about 152 bp at the warn line ahead of the Senate’s 28 Nov revenue vote; Fitch calls the Western European outlook deteriorating. EUR/USD slipped 0.20% to 1.0944 as hot US core PCE kept the dollar near 104, so the rate differential, not ECB hawkishness, is driving the pair. Italy sells BOTs on 26 Nov and medium and long BTPs on 27 Nov.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"Policy is tightening into weaker hard data: Destatis cut German Q3 growth to +0.1% q/q, yet the ECB is 87% priced for a December hike to 2.75% and Kocher expects the inflation projections to be revised up. The reserve-ratio decision was postponed past December, which removes one liquidity drain from the near term and explains part of the bank bounce that did not happen; the account of the October meeting on 26 Nov shows how united the Council was. TTF at €87.33 is lower but still above the €80 bad line, and Brent at $94.61 after the Ras Tanura attack keeps the energy channel to headline inflation open. The BoE is at 4.00% with Greene backing another rise, and the Fed is 97.8% priced for 9 Dec. Liquidity for European risk improves only if the VIX falls under 25 and TTF back under €80; neither holds now.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
